//! Why `std`'s tests live here rather than in `std`.
//!
//! **A consumer, not an insider.** This package imports `std` the way anybody
//! else does, so what it checks is the surface a user reaches. Tests written
//! inside `std/*.kh` check something slightly different, and the difference is
//! not academic: `struct({ .. })` in `std::schema` is a compiler-rewritten
//! intrinsic, and the rewrite is skipped when the name resolves to a local item
//! -- so a test inside that module cannot call the API the module exists to
//! provide. The same hazard is waiting wherever a feature knows its own name.
//!
//! **One compile for all of it.** The suite's price is per program rather than
//! per assertion: one check costs about sixteen seconds against the real `std`
//! and two hundred cost eighteen. Thirteen Rust tests for `Vector` were 193
//! seconds of CI on Ubuntu and 231 on macOS; the same assertions here are one
//! `khora test` and about seventeen. Adding an area costs a file, not a build.
//!
//! **`std` stays out of it.** Everything in `std/*.kh` is parsed and checked
//! every time anything compiles anything, so a test block there is a cost paid
//! by every build in the repository, including a user's.
//!
//! # What does not belong here
//!
//! A test that needs something a running Khora program cannot observe:
//!
//!   - a program that must **fail to compile** -- the error is the assertion,
//!     and a file that does not compile cannot hold a passing test;
//!   - a **trap**, which ends the process and would take every other block in
//!     this binary with it;
//!   - the **shape of an artifact** -- reproducibility, profiles, the cache;
//!   - anything setting an **environment variable**, which belongs to the
//!     process rather than to the test.
//!
//! Those stay in `crates/khora-codegen-llvm/tests/`, which is also where the
//! language itself is checked, by one program, in `conformance.rs`.
//!
//! # Adding an area
//!
//! A file per area, named `module std_suite::<area>;` at the top -- the package
//! finds its own files, nothing has to be declared here -- and
//! `scripts/baseline.sh` picks it up with everything else, because it runs
//! `khora test` over this package once.

module std_suite::main;

import std::core::{print};

/// A package needs an entry point; the tests are the point of this one.
///
/// `khora test` compiles the `test` blocks and runs those; `khora run` builds
/// this and prints one line, which is what makes the package buildable by
/// itself and therefore checkable by `khora check` like any other member.
pub fn main() -> Int {
  print("std_suite: run `khora test tests/std-suite`");
  0
}
